Contoso requires every resource group in its Azure estate to carry a CostCenter tag so that chargeback reporting is accurate. You must author a single custom Azure Policy definition that:

- Adds the CostCenter tag with the value inherited from the parent subscription when a resource group is created without it, rather than blocking the creation.
- Can bring existing untagged resource groups into compliance through a remediation task after the definition is assigned.

Which policy effect should you configure in the definition?

回答は不正解です
Append
説明
This answer is incorrect. Append adds a field or tag during the create or update request only, and it cannot be used with remediation tasks. Existing non-compliant resource groups would stay untagged.
DeployIfNotExists
説明
This answer is incorrect. DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource, such as a diagnostic setting or an extension, when one is missing. It is not used to alter a property or tag on the evaluated resource itself.
正解
Modify
説明
This answer is correct. Modify adds, replaces, or removes properties and tags on the resource itself, and it is the effect designed for tag governance. Modify assignments support remediation tasks, which is how previously created resource groups are brought into compliance.
Deny
説明
This answer is incorrect. Deny blocks the request outright when the condition is not met, which contradicts the requirement that creation must succeed. It also performs no correction of existing resources.
全体的な説明
Azure Policy offers several corrective effects, and choosing between them depends on whether you are changing the resource itself or deploying something alongside it. Modify operates on the evaluated resource's own properties and tags, which makes it the standard effect for tag inheritance and tag enforcement scenarios. Because Modify assignments run under a managed identity, they can execute remediation tasks that apply the change to resources created before the assignment existed. DeployIfNotExists shares that remediation capability but targets a separate related resource rather than a property on the evaluated one. Append is limited to the request path at create or update time and offers no remediation, while Deny blocks creation entirely and therefore fails the stated requirement.
