Contoso Ltd. has a single Azure subscription that contains 60 storage accounts. The security team wants to enable Microsoft Defender for Storage—including malware scanning and sensitive data threat detection—for only one storage account named storesensitive01, which contains regulated customer data. They do not want the plan enabled on any of the other 59 storage accounts, and they want to minimize cost while still meeting this requirement.

What should the security team do in Microsoft Defender for Cloud?

正解
Enable the Microsoft Defender for Storage plan directly on the storesensitive01 storage account at the resource level, while leaving the plan disabled at the subscription level.
説明
This answer is correct. Microsoft Defender for Storage supports being enabled at the individual resource (storage account) level independently of the subscription-level setting, allowing granular, per-account protection without incurring costs or enabling scanning on other storage accounts in the subscription.
Enable the Microsoft Defender for Servers plan on the virtual network associated with storesensitive01, and configure a custom recommendation to enable malware scanning.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Microsoft Defender for Servers protects virtual machines and servers, not storage accounts, and it has no capability to enable malware scanning or sensitive data threat detection for storage resources.
Enable the Microsoft Defender for Storage plan at the subscription level, and then configure a cost management budget that limits scanning charges to storesensitive01 only.
説明
This answer is incorrect. Cost management budgets only provide spending alerts and do not control which resources are actually scanned; enabling the plan at the subscription level would still apply protection (and generate charges) across all 60 storage accounts.
